---
import LabLayout from '../../layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'objective', label: 'Objective' },
  { id: 'command', label: 'Command' },
  { id: 'output', label: 'Output' },
  { id: 'analysis', label: 'Analysis' },
  { id: 'remediation', label: 'Remediation' },
  { id: 'lesson', label: 'Lesson' },
];
---

<LabLayout
  title="Nmap: building a full TCP inventory"
  subtitle="A full-range scan is slow. That is the point: you get a baseline you can reproduce."
  kicker="Enumeration / Nmap"
  description="A full TCP port scan with service detection against an isolated Metasploitable 2 virtual machine."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Environment', value: 'Kali Linux · VirtualBox' },
    { label: 'Target', value: 'Metasploitable 2 (isolated)' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: 'Nmap' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2026-07-16' },
  ]}
  sections={sections}
  repoUrl={`${SITE.githubUrl}/7-nmap-scan`}
  outcomes={[
    { value: '65,535', label: 'TCP ports' },
    { value: '1', label: 'isolated target' },
    { value: '1', label: 'evidence file' },
    { value: '0', label: 'production systems' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="objective" class="article-section">
    <h2>Objective</h2>
    <p>
      Build a complete TCP inventory of the lab target, including service detection, and
      save the raw output as evidence. The goal was not to exploit anything. It was to
      practice a repeatable enumeration step that comes before exploitation or remediation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="command" class="article-section">
    <h2>Command</h2>
    <div class="article-code">
      nmap -p- -sV 192.168.55.198 -oN nmap-all-ports.txt
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li><code>-p-</code> scans all 65,535 TCP ports instead of the default top 1,000.</li>
      <li><code>-sV</code> probes open ports to identify services and versions.</li>
      <li><code>-oN</code> writes a human-readable report that can be committed as evidence.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="output" class="article-section">
    <h2>Output</h2>
    <p>
      The scan produced a complete port inventory and service fingerprint. The raw output is
      stored in <code>nmap-all-ports.txt</code> in the repository. I intentionally did not
      turn this note into a list of “interesting ports” — the inventory is the artifact.
    </p>
    <p>
      Metasploitable 2 is designed to expose many services. The useful question is not
      “how many ports are open?” but “which of these services are expected, which are
      unnecessary, and which are exposed to the wrong network?”
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="analysis" class="article-section">
    <h2>Analysis</h2>
    <p>
      A full-range scan takes longer than the default top-1,000 scan, but it removes a blind
      spot. Service detection adds another pass: Nmap sends probes and compares the responses
      against its service fingerprints. The result is a starting map, not a verdict.
    </p>
    <p>
      The most useful comparison is against a baseline. If a service appears that was not
      present yesterday, that is a signal. If a management service is reachable from a
      network where it should not be, that is a bigger signal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remediation" class="article-section">
    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Close or firewall services that are not required for the lab exercise.</li>
      <li>Patch exposed services and remove default applications.</li>
      <li>Restrict management interfaces to a dedicated management network.</li>
      <li>Keep the lab segmented from the home LAN and the internet.</li>
      <li>Monitor for new listeners and unexpected service versions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lesson" class="article-section">
    <h2>Lesson</h2>
    <p>
      Enumeration is a habit, not a one-off command. Save the raw output, note the command
      and date, and compare against a baseline. And keep the scope boringly explicit: this
      scan was against an isolated VM on a host-only network, not a public target.
    </p>
  </section>
</LabLayout>
